drm: h713-afbd: audit the vsync enable against its own bookkeeping

The vsync interrupt enable, AFBD +0xc4 bit 0, has two users and one
refcount: DRM's vblank machinery (vblank_on, which the ring follower of the
passthrough plane holds through drm_crtc_vblank_get()) and the flip notifier
(h713_afbd_flip_users, one per registered consumer). Either one keeps the
bit set; only with both gone does h713_afbd_vsync_irq_update() clear it.
Reading the code proves the refcount is right as it stands: a registered
flip notifier is counted at register and unregister, and disable_vblank()
re-derives the bit from both users instead of clearing it.

What the bridge session on the HY300 Pro (18.09., logs 12-14) and the HY310
comparison (19.09., 01-idle) actually show is the state in which neither
user exists: the plane is off ("console"), so the ring follower holds no
vblank reference, and no flip notifier is registered, because the receive
driver registers it only while a V4L2 client streams and h713-tv never
streams - it drives the passthrough plane. DRM's vblank-off timer then
runs out and the bit goes to 0. That is bookkeeping working as written,
with nobody left to hold the bit. The receive driver's side of this - hold
the notifier for as long as the driver is bound - comes with 0136d.

This patch adds the audit that makes a wrong bit visible instead of
silent. The driver remembers what it last wrote (vsync_irq_on); every
update, and every poll through h713_afbd_read_flip() - the one path that
keeps running while nothing streams, because the receive driver's signal
detection uses it - compares the register with that and, when the bit is
found cleared while a consumer holds it, says so once per ratelimit window
(drm_dbg_ratelimited, category DRIVER) and sets it again. The display
firmware never writes +0xc4 (analysis A12 D: the 109 AFBD accesses of every
firmware build stop at +0x90, none in the IRQ block), and no other ARM
driver maps the window, so today the message can only report this driver's
own bookkeeping going out of step; should a writer ever turn up, setting
the bit again is still the right answer for a consumer that needs it.

No behaviour change while the bookkeeping is right: the update path writes
the same value as before, the audit finds the bit as it left it.

Follow-up to 0093, which stays byte-identical.

diff -ruN a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
@@ -185,6 +185,7 @@
 	u32 ring_y;
 	/* Under h713_afbd_flip_lock: it gates the same interrupt bit. */
 	bool vblank_on;
+	bool vsync_irq_on;	/* what the last update wrote to that bit */
 	u32 rgb_ctrl_active;
 	u32 video_ctrl_idle;
 	u32 video_c_stride_idle;
@@ -406,6 +407,31 @@
 static unsigned int h713_afbd_flip_users;
 
 /*
+ * The enable bit is audited against what this driver last wrote to it. The
+ * display firmware never touches the IRQ block (analysis A12 D: the AFBD
+ * accesses of every firmware build seen stop at +0x90), and no other ARM
+ * driver maps the window, so a bit found cleared while it should be set can
+ * only mean this driver's own bookkeeping got out of step -- worth a line.
+ * Should a writer ever turn up, setting the bit again is still the right
+ * answer: a registered consumer needs the interrupt, whoever cleared it.
+ * Runs on every update and on every poll through h713_afbd_read_flip(),
+ * which is the one path that keeps running while nothing is streaming.
+ *
+ * The caller holds h713_afbd_flip_lock.
+ */
+static void h713_afbd_vsync_irq_audit(struct h713_afbd *h)
+{
+	u8 v = readb(h->regs + AFBD_IRQ_ENABLE);
+
+	if (!h->vsync_irq_on || (v & AFBD_IRQ_VSYNC))
+		return;
+	drm_dbg_ratelimited(&h->drm,
+			    "vsync enable found cleared (+0x%03x = 0x%02x) while a consumer holds it, setting it again\n",
+			    AFBD_IRQ_ENABLE, v);
+	writeb(v | AFBD_IRQ_VSYNC, h->regs + AFBD_IRQ_ENABLE);
+}
+
+/*
  * Unmask the vsync interrupt while somebody needs it. It has two independent
  * users -- DRM's vblank machinery and the flip notifier -- and either one is
  * enough to keep it on.
@@ -416,13 +442,17 @@
  */
 static void h713_afbd_vsync_irq_update(struct h713_afbd *h)
 {
-	u8 v = readb(h->regs + AFBD_IRQ_ENABLE);
+	bool want = h->vblank_on || h713_afbd_flip_users;
+	u8 v;
 
-	if (h->vblank_on || h713_afbd_flip_users)
+	h713_afbd_vsync_irq_audit(h);
+	v = readb(h->regs + AFBD_IRQ_ENABLE);
+	if (want)
 		v |= AFBD_IRQ_VSYNC;
 	else
 		v &= ~AFBD_IRQ_VSYNC;
 	writeb(v, h->regs + AFBD_IRQ_ENABLE);
+	h->vsync_irq_on = want;
 }
 
 static bool h713_afbd_in_ring(struct h713_afbd *h, u32 addr)
@@ -520,12 +550,12 @@
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&h713_afbd_flip_lock, flags);
 	h = h713_afbd_flip_dev;
-	if (!h)
+	if (!h) {
 		ret = -EPROBE_DEFER;
-	else if (!h713_afbd_read_ring(h, &y, &c))
-		ret = -EAGAIN;
-	else
-		ret = 0;
+	} else {
+		h713_afbd_vsync_irq_audit(h);
+		ret = h713_afbd_read_ring(h, &y, &c) ? 0 : -EAGAIN;
+	}
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&h713_afbd_flip_lock, flags);
 
 	if (ret)
